ay F4, Chi W5, Play B6, Play W1, Play W4, Play F1, Play B8, Play B9, Play F4, Play W7, Play T9.\\nThe game history of the player sitting across is: Play B9, Play F3, Play T1, Play B2, Play T8, Play T6, Play B7, Play F3, Peng W3, Play W5, Play T1, Play B3, Play B8, Play W1, Play B8, Play B5, Play B9, Play F2, Play W7, Peng W6, Play T8, Play J2, Play W1.\\nThe game history of the previous player is: Play T8, Play T2, Play B5, Play F1, Play B1, Play B7, Play T7, Peng W2, Play F4, Play W3, Play J3, Play B2, Play W7, Play W8, Play T4, Play B1, Peng W4, Play F1, Chi B5, Play J3, Play B1, BuGang W2, Play F3, Play W8, Play B9, Play T6, Play B4.\\nThe followings are your valid actions:[Play B7; Play B5; Play T5; Play T6; Play B8]\\u3002\", \"reasoning\": \"\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: You are 1 meld away from winning: existing melds are T456, T567, B456; remaining hand T5 T6 B5 B7 B8 can win by forming one more meld + a pair. Best structure is keep (B7,B8) as the pair and complete a Bamboo chow with (B5,B6) or (B6,B7) or (B8,B9).  \\nReason: Discarding B5 maximizes effective outs: it leaves T5T6 (can become 456/567 with T4/T7) and B7B8 as a ready pair while drawing B6 gives immediate win (make B678 with B6, pair B7B8), and B9 also gives a win path (B789 with B9 if later find B6/7 adjustments). Keeping B5 instead reduces flexibility because B5 overlaps your already-fixed B456 meld and B6 availability is low (only 1 unseen), so better to free the hand and aim for B6/T4/T7.  \\nAnswer: Play B5\"}"}
